it's been one year since restless  individual traders shaken by the  coronavirus pandemic started what they  thought would be a revolution against  wall street most people remember it is  the story of small investors banding  together to beat the street and make  huge profits but that's not exactly what  happened the truth is the meme stock  craze and the band of angry reddit  traders brought the hedge funds big  banks and professional money managers  a big payday but the story doesn't end  there the battle for the soul of the  stock market and the future of investing  that's still going on i'm going to  explain how the so-called revolution  really lined wall street's pockets how  the market has changed since january  2021  and what the new world of investing  means for the stock market going forward  we're going to dig into all that but  first let me remind you of how we got  here  build as the finance world's david  versus goliath most people first started  hearing about meme stocks in january  2021 as gamestop stock started taking  off the army of traders who had gathered  on the wall street bets subreddit had  discovered something of a cheat code  they identified and bought stocks with  high levels of short interest that hedge  funds were lining up to bet would fail  gamestop was one of the main stocks the  subreddit had identified at the time it  had been rising steadily through 2020 as  the overall market recovered but during  the month of january 2021 the stocks  price rose by 1600 percent for the hedge  funds there were some big losses melvin  capital a hedge fund with 12.5 billion  dollars in assets last january lost more  than half of its clients money short  selling gamestop the company had huge  uncovered bets the gamestop stock would  fall in price then on january 28th robin  hood and many of the most popular retail  trading brokerages announced they would  halt buying in gamestop and several  other stocks that retail had been buying  that seemed to change everything furious  retail traders blamed robin hood and a  cabal of hedge funds politicians and  money managers for conspiring to stick  it to the little guy this is the part of  the story where most people stop paying  attention but it's where the real story  of the retail trading revolution begins  they thought that they'd found a great  money making scheme and a way to blow  multi-billion dollar holes in some of  the biggest funds on wall street my  colleague spencer jacob who edits wsj's  heard on the street column wrote a book  called the revolution that wasn't that  just isn't what happened they mostly did  not make money and they poured a lot of  money into wall street's pocket the  retail trading bonanza led to record  profits for firms like goldman sachs and  morgan stanley even robin hood raked in  massive gains how did this happen  traders were buying options and buying  and selling stocks multiple times a day  every trade that's made is routed  through a market maker that pays  brokerage firms a fee this is how  companies like robinhood that don't  charge users a direct fee for trading  make their money companies like citadel  securities are the market makers they  take a fraction of the cost of the trade  and they pocket that it's a tiny  percentage of ascent but when there are  millions of trades happening every hour  that adds up to a lot of money at the  end of the day more trades also means  more money flowing through investment  banks like goldman jp morgan and  deutsche bank and while melvin capital  and a few others suffered most hedge  funds did very very well many retail  traders did pretty well too but  eventually the whole thing fell apart  between january 28th and  a week later  gamestop lost almost 90 percent of its  value but the story isn't over many  retail traders were first drawn to  trading by the craze and meme stocks  now they're driving a few big changes to  the way the stock market operates  perhaps the biggest we've seen is the  growth in options trading which reached  record levels in 2021 thanks to a huge  increase in activity from retail  analysts say this has been a big driver  of stock prices retail used to just be  along for the ride while big  institutions and hedge funds drove the  market that's changing we've almost  seemed like a confident shift kali cox  market analyst at etoro has been keeping  a close eye on what's happening retail  investors kind of know their power they  know their voice they know that they  they're able to move markets and  community they know how these markets  work  they know that you know the fed is  really engaged right now  and they've seen a market that you know  basically rewards those who kind of stay  in it the surge in trading by everyday  investors and the meme stock revolution  have even gotten lawmakers and  regulators to look into issues that some  retail traders have been screaming about  for years all this attention was the  result of these headlines but also the  fact that retail traders are becoming  too large to ignore jp morgan estimates  that individual traders have accounted  for over a third of stock market trading  several times over the past 18 months  they made up 40 percent of the trading  on peak days and retail bought seven  times more stocks in 2021 than they did  in 2019 young people are getting more  involved in the markets and that trend  doesn't look like it's going to stop  six in 10 people between the ages of 25  and 34 so they put more money in  investments over the last year this has  made wall street's professional class a  little nervous about getting burned  again according to a recent survey 85 of  hedge funds and 42 percent of  professional asset managers are now  tracking retail trading message boards  so while the big banks corporations and  wealthy financiers may have won the  first round it's clear that the reddit  revolution has made its mark the big  question is what's next retail traders  are showing no signs of taking their  ball and going home so far these traders  seem interested in risky bets like  options high-flying stocks and crypto  currencies but data also show that  retail traders are buying old-fashioned  index funds and blue chip companies like  apple and microsoft that shows that  retail traders are taking more risk but  they're also becoming more practical  investors who are thinking about  investing for the long term one big  difference has been a lack of fear  before the great financial crisis retail  was kind of seen as the money the dumb  money the money that would sell quickly  once something went wrong today's retail  trader is seen that when prices fall  it's not a time to be fearful and hide  it's time to buy the dip retail's top 8  buying days have all come when the s p  500 was down by at least 1.3 percent  traditional wall street has been  redefined in recent years by high  frequency trading algorithms and  artificial intelligence it's now being  upended by individual traders who have  some new ideas about how to buy and sell  it remains to be seen how this new force  will respond when stock prices fall and  stay down  and what that will mean for the market  and the economy  if you enjoyed this video hit the like  button and if you're interested in  markets and want to watch and learn more  subscribe to this channel to keep up  with my latest in the words of brooklyn  legend fabulous respect ain't earn twice  once you lose it it's gone and it's lame  when your money's right but you're using  it wrong that's it for now i'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

